`find_screen(1479, 91)` returns `None` because both screens A and B contain that point, so `find_closest_screen` builds `x_match` and `y_match` and passes their concatenation to `_find_closest_closest`. From the test data, `x_match` is `[A, B]` and `y_match` is `[C, A, B]`, so the initial candidates are `[A, B, C, A, B]`. The filter `x < s.x + s.width and y < s.y + s.height` keeps all five of those screens, since each screen's right edge is greater than 1479 and each bottom edge is greater than 91. `_find_closest_closest` then compares squared distances from `(1479, 91)` to each screen center; the smallest value is for screen B, with distance `29529.25`, so that is the final `closest_distance`.

